import LeanCompCert.Ports.DirichletLadder

/-!
# The Dirichlet zero-ladder checker, end to end, at source parameters

`Ports.DirichletLadder` at Platt's own Theorem 7.1 schedule
(`sourceCfg`: `T_q = max(10^8/q, 200 + 7.5*10^7/q)` for even `q`), on the
conjugate pair of primitive characters modulo `q = 400000`.  That modulus is
where the source height is smallest — `T_q = 387.5`, so `ceil(387.5/5) = 78`
blocks per character, 156 records — which is why it is the one modulus of the
whole roster whose real schedule fits in one artifact.

The program seeds its own record stream and byte weight table, so the artifact
is self-contained and its exit status depends on nothing outside the binary.

What is proved and what the binary corroborates are, as always, different:

* proved — `Ports.DirichletLadder.ladderProgram_compiled` and
  `seededWith_wf`: for a well-formed base address the compiled CCIR trace
  leaves the program's denotation in the output register, at every stream
  length and every configuration;
* proved — that the denotation *is* the reference checker `refViolations`, by
  kernel evaluation on a conforming stream **and on five mutated streams that
  must be rejected** (see `Ports.DirichletLadder`).  Those run under `testCfg`,
  a scaled-down height schedule, because the source schedule's smallest
  character is 156 records and `denote` will not reduce in the kernel at that
  size;
* corroborated only — the numeric value below at source parameters, and its
  agreement with the independent C oracle `bench/ref_dirichlet_ladder.c` under
  `ccomp` and `gcc`.  Its exit status is not a theorem.

The sign bitmaps in this stream are synthetic.  Nothing here claims they are
the signs of `Lambda(1/2 + it, chi)`; what is exercised is every integer check
the source schedule imposes, at the source schedule's own constants.
-/

